course: dictionary
lesson: 14106
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, so you need context to know which one the writer means. The words around the word are its context. «Ella got a merit for her neat maths homework.» Here a merit is a point or star that a school gives for good work, not a kind of wealth or a type of weather.
explain: Merit can also mean being good enough to deserve something. «The prize went to Sam on merit, because his story was the best.» On merit means because someone deserves it, not because of luck or who they know. Try swapping in "because he deserved it" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Merit can be a verb too. «The brave lifeguard merited a medal.» To merit something means to deserve it. When you meet a word you know, check whether it is being used as a noun or a verb, because that changes what it means.
fact: Merit means the quality of being good and deserving praise or a reward.
fact: At school, a merit is a point, star or certificate given for good work or good behaviour.
fact: The merits of something are its good points, so you can weigh the merits and drawbacks of an idea.
fact: To merit something is to deserve it.
fact: Phrases to know: «on merit» means because someone deserves it, and «merit award» is a prize for good work.
exercise: «Maya earned a merit for her kind work in the library.» What is a merit here? | A long walk home | A heavy rainstorm | A loud noise in the hall | *A school star or point for good work
exercise: «The class talked about the merits and drawbacks of the new playground.» What are the merits of the playground? | Its rules about running | Its wrong colour | Its tall walls | *Its good points
exercise: «Ben was chosen on merit, because he had worked the hardest.» What does on merit mean? | By chance, with a coin | *Because he deserved it | Because it was his birthday | Because his friend asked for him
test: «The team merited a prize after winning every game.» What does merited mean? | *Deserved | Forgot | Lost | Argued
test: «The old queen was loved for her merit, for her kindness and wisdom.» What is merit in this sentence? | A thick winter coat | *Goodness that deserves praise | A kind of cake | Noisy anger
exam: «The head teacher said the essay had real merit, so it would be read to the whole school.» What does real merit mean here? | A loud voice | A real mistake | *Real quality that deserves praise | Wrong spelling
